	\item We follow \cite{Bat93} to define the \textit{jacobian ring} $R_f$ and the \textit{(interior) module} $R_{Int,f}$ over $R_f$ (see section \ref{section_constr_jac_ring}) and of course use the identification (\ref{equation_mixed_Hodge_components_of_Z_f_of_min_wei}).
\end{itemize}

In section (\ref{section_smooth_and_stable_points}) we study $\Delta$ up to replacing by an integral lattice vector. In particular we effort $(0,...,0) \in \Int(\Delta)$  to build a smooth quotient of $B$ by the torus $T$. \leavevmode \\ \\
Concerning the cokernel $\coker(\kappa_{f})$ the situation might get more complicated. In (\cite{Mav03}) there were made some efforts for $Y_f$ a K3 hypersurface (so called non-polynomial deformations), realizing these deformations as deformations of complete intersections in a single big toric variety, but is seems unlikely that this representation is useful in order to compute the missing vector space $\ker(\Phi_{f \vert{\coker(\kappa_f)}})$. In (\cite{BrGr12}) there were made some efforts to combine the Hodge components and their computation to the generic Picard number of the hypersurface.

