Then, for an element \(g=g_{\Gamma}(f)\cdot x^w\in\ker(d\mathcal{P}_f^1)\), one distinguishes cases according to the scalar product with the inner facet normal \(n_{\Gamma}\):
\[
\langle w,n_{\Gamma}\rangle=\begin{cases}
0,&\Leftrightarrow\ g\equiv 0,\\
-1,&\Leftrightarrow\ g\in\ker(\kappa_f),\\
\le -2,&\text{exceptional cases.}
\end{cases}
\]
Moreover, “\(\langle w,n_{\Gamma'}\rangle\ge 0\) for \(\Gamma'\ne\Gamma\)” by the main theorem. The “last case” (\(\langle w,n_{\Gamma}\rangle\le -2\)) “causes \(\Phi_{f\vert{Im\,\kappa_f}}\) to be not injective,” hence “the infinitesimal Torelli theorem (ITT) fails for such hypersurfaces \(Y_f\).”

In the projective-space case, for “smooth projective hypersurfaces \(Y_f\subset\mathbb{P}^n\) of degree \(\ge n+2\), where \(n\ge 3\),” one uses that “the Kodaira–Spencer map \(\kappa_f\) is surjective” to obtain “a new proof of the ITT for such nondegenerate hypersurfaces.”

For the final corollary (when \(n=3\)), the argument is: “we construct a \(3\)-dimensional empty polytope \(Q\) with \(6\) vertices,” namely “the convex span of an empty triangle with vertex \((0,0,0)\) and this triangle dilated by \(w\).” Then one “use[s] a theorem of White on empty \(3\)-simplices to deduce \(|Q\cap M|\ge 7\) under the assumption \(\langle w,n_{\Gamma}\rangle\le -2\), contradiction,” ruling out the exceptional case and yielding injectivity.

